{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d2f71c0-aaeb-4036-8182-9e9f82ad1029","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Flip two fair, independent coins. The first flip branches into heads or tails, each with probability one half.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Two fair coin flips"},{"op":"figure","id":"tree","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.5,6.5],"y":[-3.8,3.8],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"heads","at":[2.5,2],"label":"H"},{"kind":"point","id":"tails","at":[2.5,-2],"label":"T"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"start"},"to":{"at":"heads"},"label":"1/2"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"start"},"to":{"at":"tails"},"label":"1/2"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d2f71c0-aaeb-4036-8182-9e9f82ad1029","step":{"id":"s2","script":"From either first result, the second coin again has equal chances. Following a complete path gives one ordered outcome.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"branches","on":"tree","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"hh","at":[5.7,3],"label":"HH"},{"kind":"point","id":"ht","at":[5.7,1],"label":"HT"},{"kind":"point","id":"th","at":[5.7,-1],"label":"TH"},{"kind":"point","id":"tt","at":[5.7,-3],"label":"TT"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"heads"},"to":{"at":"hh"},"label":"1/2"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"heads"},"to":{"at":"ht"},"label":"1/2"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"tails"},"to":{"at":"th"},"label":"1/2"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"tails"},"to":{"at":"tt"},"label":"1/2"}]},{"op":"text","id":"order","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"HT means heads, then tails."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d2f71c0-aaeb-4036-8182-9e9f82ad1029","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Multiply probabilities along a path. Every complete outcome has probability one quarter; the four outcome probabilities add to one.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"multiply","anchor":{"el":"order","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P(HH)=\\frac12\\times\\frac12=\\frac14"},{"op":"figure","id":"probabilities","on":"tree","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[6.25,3],"text":"¼","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[6.25,1],"text":"¼","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[6.25,-1],"text":"¼","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[6.25,-3],"text":"¼","size":"small"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d2f71c0-aaeb-4036-8182-9e9f82ad1029","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Exactly one head occurs on HT or TH. These distinct outcomes cannot happen together, so add their probabilities.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"highlight","on":"tree","marks":[{"kind":"circle","center":{"at":"ht"},"r":0.28},{"kind":"circle","center":{"at":"th"},"r":0.28}]},{"op":"equation","id":"onehead","anchor":{"el":"multiply","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P(\\text{exactly one head})=\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What is the probability of getting at least one head?","expected_gist":"HH, HT, and TH qualify, so add three quarters: 3/4."}}}
